# box's one internal seed (#214). There is no role axis and no second shape:
# every ordinary mint renders this file, and what a box BECOMES is installed
# inside it, by its operator, after box has handed it over.
# KEY="value" only. Parsed against an allowlist, never sourced; there is no
# key for a network or a security flag, on purpose — the shared box-net
# profile is the placement contract and no template can weaken it. There is
# no key for convergence either, for the same shape of reason: box provisions
# and manages VMs, and it does not converge them.
# BOX_USER is the tenant user, and 'box new --user <name>' overrides it before
# cloud-init is rendered and the box is stamped. bin/box spells the same
# default: BOX_IMAGE and BOX_USER are required keys, so this file cannot leave
# it blank, and cmd_new must have a user before load_template has read one.
# The two agree deliberately and are asserted to agree by test/cli.sh.
# /tmp is RAM and swap is provisioned, both in user-data.yaml and both
# deliberately DECOUPLED from the memory the box is minted at (#178): systemd
# sizes /tmp at 50% of it, so raising that figure raises the scratch ceiling
# with it and doubles the RAM scratch can eat — there is no value here that
# fixes that, only values that make it slower to notice. The seed therefore
# caps /tmp at a fixed 1GiB and lays a 4GiB swapfile on the disk; change
# either there, not here.
# The VM is this box's trust boundary: it runs untrusted code, so no
# silent container fallback when KVM is absent
# (BOX_NO_CONTAINER_FALLBACK, #175).
BOX_DESCRIPTION="Blank Debian 13 tenant — box mints it, you converge it"
BOX_IMAGE="images:debian/13/cloud"
BOX_USER="dev"
BOX_NO_CONTAINER_FALLBACK="1"
